//? Title: The Berkeley Algorithm
//? Subtitle: Internal clock synchronization with a fault-tolerant average, its skew bound derived and tested on a sensor network whose quartz clocks drift with the 1973 New York summer temperatures, using datamunge's algorithms::simulate_berkeley_network
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
The Berkeley algorithm keeps a group of computer clocks close to one another without any
reference clock. A master polls every clock, averages the readings that agree within a
window, and tells each clock how far it is from that average. We derive Cristian's bound on
the reading error, the skew bound \(2\varepsilon+\Delta\rho R\) between rounds, and the
conditions a fault-tolerant window must meet. We test them on a simulated sensor network whose
quartz clocks drift with the daily temperatures in `dm::datasets::airquality()`, New York in
the summer of 1973, plus one broken clock. Hourly rounds keep the good clocks within 61 ms of
each other, inside the 91 ms bound, and reject the broken clock every round. The group as a
whole still loses 27 s against true time, the mean of its clocks' errors. With 15-minute rounds
the broken clock never gets far enough ahead to be noticed, and it drags the whole network 375 s
fast.
)

\toc

> Clocks that drift

A computer clock counts the oscillations of a quartz crystal. The crystal's frequency is never
exactly right, so the clock gains or loses time at a *rate error* \(\rho\), measured in parts per
million (ppm). One ppm is 86 ms a day. The cheap 32.768 kHz tuning-fork crystals in watches and
sensor boards are cut to be exact at a *turnover temperature* \(T_0\approx25\,^\circ\)C. Away from
it they always run slow, following the parabolic law

$$
\rho(T)=-\beta\,(T-T_0)^2,\qquad\beta\approx0.034\ \text{ppm}/^\circ\text{C}^2,
$$
\alttext(rho of T equals minus beta times T minus T0 squared, with beta about 0.034 ppm per degree Celsius squared.)

so a clock at 5 °C loses 13.6 ppm, more than a second a day. `algorithms::crystal_drift_ppm`
implements this law. Clocks in different places therefore drift apart. A distributed system
can ask for *external* synchronization (every clock close to true time, which needs a reference
such as GPS or an atomic clock) or *internal* synchronization (every clock close to every other,
whatever the true time). The Berkeley algorithm (Gusella and Zatti 1989) provides internal
synchronization without any reference clock.

>> Weather as a clock error

`dm::datasets::airquality()` holds daily readings in New York from 1 May to 30 September 1973,
including the maximum temperature in °F. We use it to drive a small network of five
sensor clocks with tuning-fork crystals:

| node | where | temperature |
| --- | --- | --- |
| 0 (master) | indoors | 21 °C |
| 1 | outdoors in the shade | the day's temperature |
| 2 | outdoors in the sun | the day's temperature + 8 °C |
| 3 | in a basement | 15 °C |
| 4 | broken | runs 150 ppm fast |

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/berkeley_drift.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

namespace alg = dm::algorithms;

int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const auto air = dm::datasets::airquality();
  const auto& fahrenheit = air.get_column("Temp").values();
  std::vector<double> day, outdoor;
  for (std::size_t d = 0; d < fahrenheit.size(); ++d) {
    day.push_back(static_cast<double>(d));
    outdoor.push_back((fahrenheit[d] - 32.0) * 5.0 / 9.0);
  }
  const std::vector<std::string> names = {"indoors", "shade", "sun", "basement"};
  std::vector<std::vector<double>> drift(4);
  for (const double t : outdoor) {
    drift[0].push_back(alg::crystal_drift_ppm(21.0));
    drift[1].push_back(alg::crystal_drift_ppm(t));
    drift[2].push_back(alg::crystal_drift_ppm(t + 8.0));
    drift[3].push_back(alg::crystal_drift_ppm(15.0));
  }
  std::print("{}\n\n", air | dm::summarise(dm::agg::n("days"), dm::agg::min("Temp", "coolest (°F)"),
                                            dm::agg::median("Temp", "median (°F)"), dm::agg::max("Temp", "hottest (°F)"))
                         | dm::to_md(0));
  std::print("| node | mean drift (ppm) | range (ppm) | lost over the season (s) |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  for (std::size_t i = 0; i < 4; ++i) {
    const auto [lo, hi] = std::ranges::minmax(drift[i]);
    const double mean = std::accumulate(drift[i].begin(), drift[i].end(), 0.0) / drift[i].size();
    std::print("| {} | {:.2f} | {:.2f} to {:.2f} | {:.1f} |\n", names[i], mean, lo, hi, -mean * 1e-6 * 86400.0 * drift[i].size());
  }

  const std::vector<dm::RGB> colours = {{17, 24, 39}, {37, 99, 235}, {217, 119, 6}, {22, 163, 74}};
  auto temp = dm::graphics::RPlot::create()
                  .lines(day, outdoor, "shade", colours[1], 1.5)
                  .abline_h(25.0, dm::RGB{148, 163, 184}, 1.0)
                  .title("Daily maximum temperature, New York 1973")
                  .x_label("day (0 = 1 May)")
                  .y_label("°C (line: crystal turnover, 25 °C)");
  auto rates = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
  for (std::size_t i = 0; i < 4; ++i) rates.lines(day, drift[i], names[i], colours[i], 1.5);
  rates.y_limits(-14, 7)
      .title("Rate error of each good clock")
      .x_label("day (0 = 1 May)")
      .y_label("ppm (negative: slow)");
  dm::graphics::RLayout::create(1, 2).add(temp).add(rates).size(1200, 440).save_svg("build/berkeley_drift.svg");
}
```
