\caption(Left: the daily maximum temperature in New York, May to September 1973, with the crystals' 25 °C turnover temperature. Right: the resulting rate errors of the four good clocks.)
\alttext(Left: the daily maximum temperature in degrees Celsius from day 0 to 152, starting between 13 and 23 in May, rising above 25 from about day 35, peaking near 36 at day 120, and falling back to around 20 to 25 in September; a grey line marks 25. Right: rate errors in ppm. Indoors is a flat black line at minus 0.54 and the basement a flat green line at minus 3.4. The shade clock (blue) stays between 0 and minus 4.6, dipping in the cool days of May and September. The sun clock (orange) is near 0 in May and falls as low as minus 12.4 around day 118.)

The daily maximum ranges from 56 to 97 °F (13 to 36 °C), with a median of 79 °F. The
parabolic law makes every clock run slow, and the further its temperature is from 25 °C the
slower it runs. The indoor master loses 0.54 ppm, steadily, and the basement clock 3.4 ppm. The
shade clock is nearly exact on warm days but loses up to 4.6 ppm on the coolest. The clock in
the sun suffers on the hottest days, losing up to 12.4 ppm. Over the season, free-running, they
would lose 7 to 45 s each.

> The algorithm

>> One round

One node, the *master*, polls the others. For each node \(i\) it sends a request, which takes
\(a_i\) seconds to arrive. The node replies with its clock reading, which takes \(b_i\) seconds to
come back. The master cannot see \(a_i\) and \(b_i\) separately, only the round trip \(a_i+b_i\) on its
own clock, so it assumes the reply spent half the round trip in transit. The reading was taken
\(b_i\) seconds before the reply arrived, so the estimate of node \(i\)'s offset from the master is
wrong by

$$
e_i=\frac{a_i+b_i}{2}-b_i=\frac{a_i-b_i}{2},\qquad |e_i|\le\varepsilon=\frac{d_{\max}-d_{\min}}{2},
$$
\alttext(e i equals a i plus b i over 2 minus b i, which is a i minus b i over 2, at most epsilon equals d max minus d min over 2 in size.)

where every one-way delay lies in \([d_{\min},d_{\max}]\). This is Cristian's bound (Cristian 1989).
The master then averages the estimates and sends each node the *adjustment* that takes it to
the average. It sends adjustments rather than times, so the delay of that message does not
matter.

A broken clock would drag a plain average with it. Gusella and Zatti's *fault-tolerant average*
uses only the largest group of estimates that fit in a window of width \(w\). Clocks outside the
group are left out of the average but are still adjusted. `algorithms::berkeley_sync_window`
finds that group by sliding the window along the sorted estimates.

>> The skew bound

Just after a round, node \(i\) sits at the average plus its estimation error, so any two clocks in
the group differ by at most \(2\varepsilon\). Until the next round, \(R\) seconds later, two clocks
with rate errors \(\rho_i\) and \(\rho_j\) separate by \((\rho_i-\rho_j)R\). The *skew*, the largest
difference between good clocks, therefore obeys

$$
S\;\le\;2\varepsilon+\Delta\rho\,R,\qquad\Delta\rho=\max_i\rho_i-\min_i\rho_i.
$$
\alttext(The skew S is at most 2 epsilon plus delta rho times R, where delta rho is the largest rate error minus the smallest.)

The bound also limits the choice of window. The window is applied to the master's *estimates*,
each up to \(\varepsilon\) wrong, so the good clocks' estimates can spread by \(S+2\varepsilon\). All of
them fit only if

$$
w\;\ge\;4\varepsilon+\Delta\rho\,R.
$$
\alttext(w is at least 4 epsilon plus delta rho times R.)

A faulty clock with rate error \(\rho_f\) gets ahead of the group by about \((\rho_f-\rho)R\) between
rounds, and it is rejected only if that lead, less the estimation errors and the group's own
spread, exceeds \(w\). Short rounds keep the good clocks close together, but they also give a
faulty clock little time to stand out. Even when a faulty clock is accepted, it moves the
average by at most \(w/m\) for a group of \(m\).

Nothing in a round refers to true time. The group follows its own average, so it drifts at the
*mean* rate error of the clocks it averages, and over a long period it can be far from true time.

> A summer of synchronization

`algorithms::simulate_berkeley_network` runs the five clocks for the 153 days. One-way delays
are uniform between 2 and 50 ms, so \(\varepsilon=24\) ms, and the window is \(w=0.25\) s. First we
compare free-running clocks with hourly Berkeley rounds.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/berkeley_season.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

namespace alg = dm::algorithms;

int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const auto air = dm::datasets::airquality();
  std::vector<std::vector<double>> drift(5);
  for (const double f : air.get_column("Temp").values()) {
    const double t = (f - 32.0) * 5.0 / 9.0;
    drift[0].push_back(alg::crystal_drift_ppm(21.0));
    drift[1].push_back(alg::crystal_drift_ppm(t));
    drift[2].push_back(alg::crystal_drift_ppm(t + 8.0));
    drift[3].push_back(alg::crystal_drift_ppm(15.0));
    drift[4].push_back(150.0);
  }
  alg::BerkeleyNetworkOptions options;
  options.round_interval = 3600.0;
  options.window = 0.25;
  options.synchronize = false;
  const auto free = alg::simulate_berkeley_network(drift, options);
  options.synchronize = true;
  const auto sync = alg::simulate_berkeley_network(drift, options);

  const std::vector<std::string> names = {"indoors (master)", "shade", "sun", "basement", "broken"};
  const std::size_t last = sync.time_days.size() - 1;
  std::print("| node | offset after 153 days, free (s) | with hourly Berkeley rounds (s) | rounds rejected |\n"
             "| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  for (std::size_t i = 0; i < 5; ++i)
    std::print("| {} | {:.1f} | {:.2f} | {} |\n", names[i], free.before[i][last], sync.after[i][last],
               std::ranges::count(sync.included[i], 0));
  double worst = 0.0;
  for (std::size_t k = 0; k < sync.time_days.size(); ++k) worst = std::max(worst, sync.skew_before[k]);
  std::print("\n{} rounds; largest spread of the agreeing clocks just before a round: {:.0f} ms.\n",
             sync.time_days.size(), 1000.0 * worst);

  const std::vector<dm::RGB> colours = {{17, 24, 39}, {37, 99, 235}, {217, 119, 6}, {22, 163, 74}};
  auto left = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
  auto right = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
  std::vector<double> days;
  for (std::size_t k = 0; k < sync.time_days.size(); k += 24) days.push_back(sync.time_days[k]);
  for (std::size_t i = 0; i < 4; ++i) {
    std::vector<double> f, s;
    for (std::size_t k = 0; k < sync.time_days.size(); k += 24) {
      f.push_back(free.before[i][k]);
      s.push_back(sync.after[i][k]);
    }
    left.lines(days, f, names[i], colours[i], 1.8);
    right.lines(days, s, names[i], colours[i], 1.8);
  }
  left.y_limits(-50, 14).title("Free-running clocks").x_label("day").y_label("offset from true time (s)");
  right.y_limits(-50, 14).title("Hourly Berkeley rounds").x_label("day").y_label("offset from true time (s)");
  dm::graphics::RLayout::create(1, 2).add(left).add(right).size(1200, 440).save_svg("build/berkeley_season.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| node | offset after 153 days, free (s) | with hourly Berkeley rounds (s) | rounds rejected |
| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| indoors (master) | -7.2 | -27.33 | 0 |
| shade | -12.5 | -27.34 | 0 |
| sun | -44.7 | -27.32 | 0 |
| basement | -44.9 | -27.34 | 0 |
| broken | 1982.9 | -27.33 | 3672 |

3672 rounds; largest spread of the agreeing clocks just before a round: 61 ms.
\image(figures/berkeley_season.svg)
// result_end

\caption(Offsets of the four good clocks from true time over the season. Left: free-running. Right: with a Berkeley round every hour, where the four lines lie on top of each other. The broken clock, 150 ppm fast, is off the scale when free-running and is not drawn.)
\alttext(Left: offsets of the four good clocks over 153 days, all starting at 0 and falling: indoors steadily to minus 7 seconds, shade in steps to minus 12.5, basement steadily to minus 45, and sun slowly until day 30 then faster, ending at minus 45. Right: with hourly rounds the four lines coincide as one line falling from 0 to minus 27 seconds, a little faster in the hot spells.)

Without synchronization the clocks end the season up to 38 s apart, and the broken clock is
33 minutes fast. With a Berkeley round every hour, the four good clocks end within 20 ms of one
another, and over the 3,672 rounds their spread just before a round never exceeded 61 ms. The
broken clock was rejected from the average in every round. Each round it was still told its
adjustment, so it too stays with the group, 150 ppm × 1 h = 0.54 s ahead at worst.

What Berkeley does not fix is true time. The four lines on the right fall together, to
\(-27.3\) s after 153 days. The group runs at the mean rate error of its clocks,
\(-(0.54+0.94+3.38+3.40)/4=-2.07\) ppm, which over 153 days is exactly 27.3 s. Internal
synchronization makes the clocks agree; it cannot make them right.

>> Round interval, window and skew

The bound says the skew grows linearly with the round interval. We test it for rounds every 15
minutes, hour, 6 hours and day. In each case we measure the largest spread of the four good
clocks just before a round, against \(2\varepsilon+\Delta\rho R\), where \(\Delta\rho\) is taken from
each day's rates. We also run a plain average with no window.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/berkeley_skew.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

namespace alg = dm::algorithms;

int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const auto air = dm::datasets::airquality();
  std::vector<std::vector<double>> drift(5);
  for (const double f : air.get_column("Temp").values()) {
    const double t = (f - 32.0) * 5.0 / 9.0;
    drift[0].push_back(alg::crystal_drift_ppm(21.0));
    drift[1].push_back(alg::crystal_drift_ppm(t));
    drift[2].push_back(alg::crystal_drift_ppm(t + 8.0));
    drift[3].push_back(alg::crystal_drift_ppm(15.0));
    drift[4].push_back(150.0);
  }
  const std::size_t days = drift[0].size();
  std::vector<double> spread_rate(days);  // Delta rho of the good clocks, per day
  for (std::size_t d = 0; d < days; ++d) {
    double lo = 1e9, hi = -1e9;
    for (std::size_t i = 0; i < 4; ++i) {
      lo = std::min(lo, drift[i][d]);
      hi = std::max(hi, drift[i][d]);
    }
    spread_rate[d] = hi - lo;
  }

  std::print("| round interval | window (s) | worst good-clock skew (ms) | bound \\(2\\varepsilon+\\Delta\\rho R\\) (ms) "
             "| rounds rejecting a good clock | rounds accepting the broken clock | agreed time after 153 days (s) |\n"
             "| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  const auto run = [&](double interval, double window, const std::string& label, bool plot) {
    alg::BerkeleyNetworkOptions o;
    o.round_interval = interval;
    o.window = window;
    const auto t = alg::simulate_berkeley_network(drift, o);
    double worst = 0.0, bound = 0.0;
    std::size_t good_rejected = 0, broken_accepted = 0;
    std::vector<double> x, skew, line;
    for (std::size_t k = 0; k < t.time_days.size(); ++k) {
      double lo = 1e9, hi = -1e9;
      for (std::size_t i = 0; i < 4; ++i) {
        lo = std::min(lo, t.before[i][k]);
        hi = std::max(hi, t.before[i][k]);
      }
      const std::size_t d = std::min(days - 1, static_cast<std::size_t>(t.time_days[k] - 1e-9));
      const double b = (o.max_delay - o.min_delay) + spread_rate[d] * 1e-6 * interval;
      if (k > 0) {
        worst = std::max(worst, hi - lo);
        bound = std::max(bound, b);
      }
      good_rejected += !(t.included[0][k] && t.included[1][k] && t.included[2][k] && t.included[3][k]);
      broken_accepted += t.included[4][k] == 1;
      if (plot && t.time_days[k] >= 60.0 && t.time_days[k] <= 62.0) {
        x.push_back(t.time_days[k]);
        skew.push_back(1000.0 * (hi - lo));
        line.push_back(1000.0 * b);
      }
    }
    double agreed = 0.0;
    for (std::size_t i = 0; i < 4; ++i) agreed += t.after[i].back() / 4.0;
    std::print("| {} | {} | {:.0f} | {:.0f} | {} | {} | {:.1f} |\n", label, window > 0 ? std::format("{:.2f}", window) : "none",
               1000.0 * worst, 1000.0 * bound, good_rejected, broken_accepted, agreed);
    return std::tuple{x, skew, line};
  };
  run(900.0, 0.25, "15 min", false);
  run(900.0, 0.07, "15 min", false);
  const auto [x, skew, line] = run(3600.0, 0.25, "1 hour", true);
  run(21600.0, 0.25, "6 hours", false);
  run(86400.0, 0.25, "1 day", false);
  run(86400.0, 1.5, "1 day", false);
  run(3600.0, 0.0, "1 hour", false);

  dm::graphics::RPlot::create()
      .lines(x, skew, "good-clock skew", dm::RGB{37, 99, 235}, 1.5)
      .points(x, skew, "", dm::RGB{37, 99, 235}, 4.0)
      .lines(x, line, "bound 2ε + Δρ R", dm::RGB{220, 38, 38}, 1.5)
      .y_limits(0, 120)
      .title("Hourly rounds, days 60 to 62 (30 June to 2 July)")
      .x_label("day")
      .y_label("ms")
      .size(900, 420)
      .save_svg("build/berkeley_skew.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| round interval | window (s) | worst good-clock skew (ms) | bound \(2\varepsilon+\Delta\rho R\) (ms) | rounds rejecting a good clock | rounds accepting the broken clock | agreed time after 153 days (s) |
| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| 15 min | 0.25 | 50 | 59 | 0 | 14688 | 374.7 |
| 15 min | 0.07 | 50 | 59 | 4 | 0 | -27.3 |
| 1 hour | 0.25 | 61 | 91 | 0 | 0 | -27.3 |
| 6 hours | 0.25 | 263 | 304 | 4 | 0 | -27.1 |
| 1 day | 0.25 | 1044 | 1074 | 149 | 0 | -17.0 |
| 1 day | 1.50 | 1044 | 1074 | 0 | 0 | -27.3 |
| 1 hour | none | 61 | 91 | 0 | 3672 | 374.7 |
\image(figures/berkeley_skew.svg)
// result_end

\caption(The spread of the four good clocks just before each hourly round over two days, with the bound \(2\varepsilon+\Delta\rho R\) for that day's rates.)
\alttext(Over days 60 to 62, the blue skew points jump between about 5 and 41 milliseconds from round to round with no trend. The red bound line is flat at about 62 to 64 milliseconds, always above the points.)

In every configuration the worst skew of the good clocks stays below the bound, which is the
largest over the season of \(2\varepsilon+\Delta\rho R\) for the day's rates. With hourly rounds the
estimation term \(2\varepsilon=48\) ms is most of the bound, except on the hottest days, when the
sun clock's drift adds up to 45 ms. The two-day zoom shows the skew jumping between
5 and 41 ms from round to round, as the random delays reshuffle the clocks at each round. With
daily rounds the drift term dominates: the sun clock alone can lose a second a day, and the worst
skew reaches 1,044 ms against a bound of 1,074 ms.

The window rows show the trade-off between the two window conditions:

- **Daily rounds, \(w=0.25\) s.** The good clocks spread further than the window, and in 149
  of the 153 rounds a good clock was rejected. Widening the window to 1.5 s fixes this, and the
  broken clock, now 13 s ahead each day, is still rejected.
- **15-minute rounds, \(w=0.25\) s.** The broken clock gains only 135 ms between rounds, so it
  always falls inside the window. With five clocks it pulls the agreed rate up by about
  \(150/5=30\) ppm, and the network ends 375 s *fast*.
- **15-minute rounds, \(w=0.07\) s.** This rejects the broken clock every time. But the
  condition \(w\ge4\varepsilon+\Delta\rho R\approx107\) ms is violated, so a good clock was
  rejected in 4 of 14,688 rounds.
- **No window.** A plain average fails in the same way as the too-wide window, ending at
  +375 s.

> Summary

- A Berkeley round reads each clock with error at most \(\varepsilon=(d_{\max}-d_{\min})/2\) and
  moves every clock to the average, so the skew stays below \(2\varepsilon+\Delta\rho R\). On the
  simulated New York sensor network every configuration stayed inside the bound: 61 ms against
  91 ms with hourly rounds, and 1,044 ms against 1,074 ms with daily rounds.
- The fault-tolerant window (`algorithms::berkeley_sync_window`) must hold every good estimate,
  \(w\ge4\varepsilon+\Delta\rho R\), and still be outrun by a faulty clock between rounds. With
  hourly rounds and \(w=0.25\) s both held. With 15-minute rounds no window satisfied both, and a
  window wide enough for the good clocks let the broken clock pull the network 375 s fast.
- Internal is not external: with hourly rounds the clocks agreed to within 61 ms, but they all
  lost 27.3 s together, the mean rate error of the quartz crystals over the summer.

> References

- Gusella, R. and Zatti, S. (1989). The accuracy of the clock synchronization achieved by TEMPO
  in Berkeley UNIX 4.3BSD. *IEEE Transactions on Software Engineering* 15(7), 847–853.
- Cristian, F. (1989). Probabilistic clock synchronization. *Distributed Computing* 3, 146–158.
- Vig, J. R. (1992). *Introduction to Quartz Frequency Standards*. Army Research Laboratory
  technical report SLCET-TR-92-1 (the parabolic temperature law of tuning-fork crystals).
- Chambers, J. M., Cleveland, W. S., Kleiner, B. and Tukey, P. A. (1983). *Graphical Methods for
  Data Analysis*. Wadsworth (the `airquality` data).
